@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/HTML/Views/Shared/HTMLLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "HTML Form Basics";
}

<style>
    pre {
        background-color: #212529 !important;
        color: #fff !important;
        border-radius: 8px;
        padding: 16px !important;
        overflow-x: auto;
        margin-top: 12px;
    }

        pre code {
            background: transparent !important;
            color: #fff !important;
        }

    .example-box {
        border: 1px solid #dee2e6;
        border-radius: 8px;
        padding: 18px;
        margin-top: 15px;
        background-color: #f8f9fa;
    }

    .output-box {
        border-left: 4px solid #0d6efd;
        background-color: #f8f9fa;
        padding: 15px;
        margin-top: 15px;
        border-radius: 5px;
    }

    .note-box {
        border-left: 4px solid #ffc107;
        background-color: #fff8e1;
        padding: 15px;
        margin-top: 15px;
        border-radius: 5px;
    }

    table th,
    table td {
        vertical-align: middle;
    }

    .form-demo {
        background-color: #fff;
        border: 1px solid #dee2e6;
        border-radius: 8px;
        padding: 20px;
        margin-top: 15px;
    }
</style>

<main id="main" class="container py-5">

    <div class="row">

        <!-- Sidebar -->
        <div class="col-lg-3 mb-4">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/HTML/Views/Shared/HTMLTopics.cshtml")
        </div>

        <!-- Main Content -->
        <div class="col-lg-9">

            <h1 class="display-5 fw-bold mb-3">HTML Form Basics</h1>

            <p class="lead">
                HTML forms provide a structured way to collect information from users.
                This lesson covers the basic form structure, controls, labels, names,
                values, and buttons.
            </p>

            <hr class="my-4" />

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">1. What is an HTML Form?</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> element is used to create a section
                of a webpage that collects user input.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;form&gt;

    &lt;!-- Form controls go here --&gt;

&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                A form can contain text boxes, email fields, passwords, radio buttons,
                checkboxes, dropdowns, textareas, file inputs, and buttons.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">2. Basic Form Example</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;form&gt;

    &lt;label&gt;Name:&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input type="text"&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;
        Submit
    &lt;/button&gt;

&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                <div class="form-demo">
                    <label class="form-label">Name:</label>

                    <input type="text"
                           class="form-control mb-3"
                           placeholder="Enter your name" />

                    <button type="button"
                            class="btn btn-primary">
                        Submit
                    </button>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">3. Form Controls</h2>

            <p>
                Form controls are the individual elements through which users
                provide or select information.
            </p>

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped mt-3">
                <thead class="table-dark">
                    <tr>
                        <th>Control</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Text Input</td>
                        <td>Collects single-line text.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Email Input</td>
                        <td>Collects an email address.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Password Input</td>
                        <td>Collects a password.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Radio Button</td>
                        <td>Selects one option from a group.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Checkbox</td>
                        <td>Selects one or more options.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Dropdown</td>
                        <td>Selects an option from a list.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Textarea</td>
                        <td>Collects multi-line text.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Button</td>
                        <td>Performs an action such as submitting the form.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">4. The input Element</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> element is one of the most
                commonly used form elements.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="text"&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                The <code>type</code> attribute determines the kind of input control.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">5. Text Input</h2>

            <p>
                A text input allows the user to enter a single line of text.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="text"
       name="username"&gt;</code></pre>

       <div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                    <input type="text"
                           class="form-control"
                           placeholder="Enter username" />
                </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">6. Email Input</h2>

            <p>
                An email input is designed for email addresses.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="email"
       name="email"&gt;</code></pre>

       <div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                    <input type="email"
                           class="form-control"
                           placeholder="Enter email" />
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">7. Password Input</h2>

            <p>
                The password input hides the characters typed by the user.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="password"
       name="password"&gt;</code></pre>

       <div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                    <input type="password"
                           class="form-control"
                           placeholder="Enter password" />
              
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">8. Name Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>name</code> attribute identifies the form control
                when the form data is submitted.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="text"
       name="fullName"&gt;</code></pre>

            <div class="note-box">
                <strong>Important:</strong>
                Give controls meaningful <code>name</code> values when their
                data needs to be submitted.
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">9. id Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>id</code> attribute uniquely identifies an element.
                It is commonly used to connect a form control with a label.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="text"
       id="fullName"
       name="fullName"&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">10. Label Element</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> element describes a form control.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;label for="fullName"&gt;
    Full Name:
&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;input type="text"
       id="fullName"
       name="fullName"&gt;</code></pre>

       <div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                <div class="form-demo">
                    <label for="demoFullName"
                           class="form-label">
                        Full Name:
                    </label>

                    <input type="text"
                           id="demoFullName"
                           class="form-control" />
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">11. for and id Relationship</h2>

            <p>
                The value of the label's <code>for</code> attribute should match
                the input element's <code>id</code>.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;label for="email"&gt;
    Email:
&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;input type="email"
       id="email"
       name="email"&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                This association helps users understand which label belongs
                to which control.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">12. placeholder Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>placeholder</code> attribute displays a hint inside
                an empty input control.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="text"
       name="username"
       placeholder="Enter your username"&gt;</code></pre>

       <div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                    <input type="text"
                           class="form-control"
                           placeholder="Enter your username" />
            </div>

            <div class="note-box">
                <strong>Note:</strong>
                Placeholder text is a hint, not a replacement for a proper label.
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">13. value Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>value</code> attribute specifies an initial or current
                value for a form control.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="text"
       name="city"
       value="Pune"&gt;</code></pre>

       <div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                    <input type="text"
                           class="form-control"
                           value="Pune" />
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">14. required Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>required</code> attribute indicates that the user
                must provide a value before the form can be submitted.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="text"
       name="username"
       required&gt;</code></pre>

       

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">15. readonly Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>readonly</code> attribute prevents the user from
                editing the current value.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="text"
       name="studentId"
       value="STU101"
       readonly&gt;</code></pre>

       <div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                <div class="form-demo">
                    <label class="form-label">Student ID:</label>

                    <input type="text"
                           class="form-control"
                           value="STU101"
                           readonly />
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">16. disabled Attribute</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>disabled</code> attribute disables a form control.
                The user cannot interact with it.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="text"
       value="Not Available"
       disabled&gt;</code></pre>

       <div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                <div class="form-demo">
                    <input type="text"
                           class="form-control"
                           value="Not Available"
                           disabled />
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">17. Textarea</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code> element is used for
                multi-line input.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;label for="message"&gt;
    Message:
&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;textarea
    id="message"
    name="message"
    rows="5"
    cols="40"&gt;
&lt;/textarea&gt;</code></pre>


<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>
            <div class="example-box">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                <div class="form-demo">
                    <label class="form-label">
                        Message:
                    </label>

                    <textarea class="form-control"
                              rows="4"
                              placeholder="Enter your message"></textarea>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">18. Select Element</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> element creates a dropdown list.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;select name="course"&gt;

    &lt;option value="html"&gt;
        HTML
    &lt;/option&gt;

    &lt;option value="css"&gt;
        CSS
    &lt;/option&gt;

    &lt;option value="javascript"&gt;
        JavaScript
    &lt;/option&gt;

&lt;/select&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">19. Option Element</h2>

            <p>
                Each option inside a dropdown is created using the
                <code>&lt;option&gt;</code> element.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;option value="html"&gt;
    HTML
&lt;/option&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">20. Radio Buttons</h2>

            <p>
                Radio buttons allow the user to choose one option from a group.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="radio"
       id="male"
       name="gender"
       value="male"&gt;

&lt;label for="male"&gt;
    Male
&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;input type="radio"
       id="female"
       name="gender"
       value="female"&gt;

&lt;label for="female"&gt;
    Female
&lt;/label&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <div class="note-box">
                <strong>Important:</strong>
                Radio buttons belong to the same group when they use the same
                <code>name</code> value.
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">21. Checkboxes</h2>

            <p>
                Checkboxes allow the user to select one or more options.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;input type="checkbox"
       id="html"
       name="skills"
       value="html"&gt;

&lt;label for="html"&gt;
    HTML
&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;input type="checkbox"
       id="css"
       name="skills"
       value="css"&gt;

&lt;label for="css"&gt;
    CSS
&lt;/label&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">22. Submit Button</h2>

            <p>
                A button with <code>type="submit"</code> submits the form.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;button type="submit"&gt;
    Submit Form
&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">23. Reset Button</h2>

            <p>
                A button with <code>type="reset"</code> resets the form controls
                to their initial values.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;button type="reset"&gt;
    Reset Form
&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">24. Normal Button</h2>

            <p>
                A button with <code>type="button"</code> does not submit or
                reset the form automatically.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;button type="button"&gt;
    Click Me
&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">25. Complete Basic Form</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;form&gt;

    &lt;label for="name"&gt;
        Full Name:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;input type="text"
           id="name"
           name="name"
           placeholder="Enter full name"
           required&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;label for="email"&gt;
        Email:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;input type="email"
           id="email"
           name="email"
           placeholder="Enter email"
           required&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;label for="password"&gt;
        Password:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;input type="password"
           id="password"
           name="password"
           required&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;
        Submit
    &lt;/button&gt;

    &lt;button type="reset"&gt;
        Reset
    &lt;/button&gt;

&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">26. Form with Multiple Controls</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;form&gt;

    &lt;label for="name"&gt;
        Name:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;input type="text"
           id="name"
           name="name"&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;label for="email"&gt;
        Email:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;input type="email"
           id="email"
           name="email"&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;label for="course"&gt;
        Course:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;select id="course"
            name="course"&gt;

        &lt;option value="html"&gt;
            HTML
        &lt;/option&gt;

        &lt;option value="css"&gt;
            CSS
        &lt;/option&gt;

        &lt;option value="javascript"&gt;
            JavaScript
        &lt;/option&gt;

    &lt;/select&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;label for="message"&gt;
        Message:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;

    &lt;textarea
        id="message"
        name="message"
        rows="5"&gt;
    &lt;/textarea&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;
        Submit
    &lt;/button&gt;

&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">27. Form with fieldset</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> element groups related controls.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;form&gt;

    &lt;fieldset&gt;

        &lt;legend&gt;
            Personal Information
        &lt;/legend&gt;

        &lt;label for="name"&gt;
            Name:
        &lt;/label&gt;

        &lt;input type="text"
               id="name"
               name="name"&gt;

        &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

        &lt;label for="email"&gt;
            Email:
        &lt;/label&gt;

        &lt;input type="email"
               id="email"
               name="email"&gt;

    &lt;/fieldset&gt;

&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">28. legend Element</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> element provides a caption
                for a <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code>.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;fieldset&gt;

    &lt;legend&gt;
        Account Information
    &lt;/legend&gt;

    &lt;!-- Form controls --&gt;

&lt;/fieldset&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">29. Form Submission Structure</h2>

            <p>
                A common form structure includes an action URL, a method,
                form controls, and submit buttons.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;form action="/register"
      method="post"&gt;

    &lt;label for="name"&gt;
        Name:
    &lt;/label&gt;

    &lt;input type="text"
           id="name"
           name="name"
           required&gt;

    &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;
        Register
    &lt;/button&gt;

&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">30. Form Accessibility Basics</h2>

            <p>
                A good form should clearly communicate the purpose of
                every control.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Use a label for each important control.</li>
                <li>Match <code>for</code> and <code>id</code> correctly.</li>
                <li>Use meaningful names.</li>
                <li>Group related controls when appropriate.</li>
                <li>Use clear instructions.</li>
                <li>Use suitable input types.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">31. Common Mistakes</h2>

            <ul>
                <li>Forgetting the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> container.</li>
                <li>Using inputs without meaningful labels.</li>
                <li>Using incorrect <code>for</code> and <code>id</code> values.</li>
                <li>Forgetting the <code>name</code> attribute.</li>
                <li>Using the wrong input type.</li>
                <li>Using placeholder text as the only label.</li>
                <li>Forgetting submit and reset buttons.</li>
                <li>Using unrelated controls in the same group.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">32. Best Practices</h2>

            <ul>
                <li>Use semantic form elements.</li>
                <li>Use meaningful labels and names.</li>
                <li>Use appropriate input types.</li>
                <li>Keep related controls grouped together.</li>
                <li>Use HTML validation attributes where appropriate.</li>
                <li>Keep the form structure simple and clear.</li>
                <li>Design forms for different screen sizes.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">33. Practice Task</h2>

            <div class="example-box">
                <p><strong>Create a basic student form containing:</strong></p>

                <ol>
                    <li>Full Name text input.</li>
                    <li>Email input.</li>
                    <li>Password input.</li>
                    <li>Course dropdown.</li>
                    <li>Gender radio buttons.</li>
                    <li>Skills checkboxes.</li>
                    <li>Address textarea.</li>
                    <li>Submit button.</li>
                    <li>Reset button.</li>
                    <li>Required fields.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mt-5">34. Complete Practice Example</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;

&lt;head&gt;

